var e=[{topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Pathophysiology studies what goes wrong in disease. It starts with the cell: an injured cell first tries to adapt, then swells and is damaged but can still recover, and finally passes a point of no return and dies. Inflammation is the body response to that injury, and repair is what follows.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Causes of cell injury`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Hypoxia - too little oxygen. The commonest cause. Ischaemia means loss of blood supply, which cuts oxygen and nutrients together.`,`Physical agents - trauma, heat, cold, radiation, electric shock.`,`Chemicals and drugs - poisons, alcohol, paracetamol overdose.`,`Infections - bacteria, viruses, fungi, parasites.`,`Immunological reactions - allergy and autoimmune disease.`,`Genetic defects and nutritional imbalance.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Reversible or irreversible`},{kind:`para`,text:`Reversible injury means the cell can still recover if the cause is removed. The cell swells because the sodium pump fails and water enters, and fat may accumulate in it. Irreversible injury means the cell will die: the membranes break down, the mitochondria are destroyed and the nucleus disappears.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Necrosis is uncontrolled cell death after injury; a group of cells dies together, the contents leak out and inflammation follows. Apoptosis is programmed, tidy, single cell suicide that the body uses on purpose - to remove cells during development or cells that are damaged beyond use - and it causes no inflammation.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Inflammation`},{kind:`para`,text:`Inflammation is the reaction of living tissue to injury. Its five classical signs are redness (rubor), heat (calor), swelling (tumor), pain (dolor) and loss of function (functio laesa). Blood vessels widen so more blood reaches the area, they become leaky so fluid escapes into the tissue, and white cells crawl out to kill microbes and clear debris.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Acute inflammation: minutes to days, neutrophils dominate, fluid exudate is prominent.`,`Chronic inflammation: weeks to years, lymphocytes, plasma cells and macrophages dominate, with tissue destruction and scarring at the same time.`,`Exudate is protein-rich fluid from inflamed leaky vessels; transudate is protein-poor fluid caused by pressure changes alone.`,`Pus is thick fluid made of dead neutrophils, dead tissue and microbes. A walled-off collection of pus is an abscess.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Five cardinal signs`,text:`Latin quintet: Rubor, Calor, Tumor, Dolor, Functio laesa - Red, hot, swollen, painful, useless.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`One-liners`,text:`Neutrophil is the first cell of acute inflammation; macrophage is the chief cell of chronic inflammation; the epithelioid cell and giant cell make a granuloma; oedema is excess fluid in the interstitial space.`}]},{topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam level demands the adaptation vocabulary, the necrosis versus apoptosis table, the sequence of vascular and cellular events, the chemical mediators and their sources, and the two patterns of wound healing.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cellular adaptations`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Adaptation`,`Definition`,`Example`],rows:[[`Atrophy`,`Decrease in cell size and number`,`Disuse of a limb in plaster`],[`Hypertrophy`,`Increase in cell size, no new cells`,`Left ventricle in hypertension`],[`Hyperplasia`,`Increase in cell number`,`Endometrium under oestrogen`],[`Metaplasia`,`One mature cell type replaced by another`,`Bronchial columnar to squamous in smokers`],[`Dysplasia`,`Disordered growth with atypia`,`Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia`],[`Anaplasia`,`Complete loss of differentiation`,`Hallmark of malignancy`]],caption:`Metaplasia is reversible if the stimulus stops; dysplasia is pre-malignant; anaplasia is already malignant.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Necrosis versus apoptosis`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Necrosis`,`Apoptosis`],rows:[[`Trigger`,`Always pathological`,`Physiological or pathological`],[`Cells involved`,`Groups of adjacent cells`,`Single scattered cells`],[`Cell size`,`Swollen`,`Shrunken`],[`Plasma membrane`,`Lost, contents leak`,`Intact, forms blebs`],[`Nucleus`,`Pyknosis, karyorrhexis, karyolysis`,`Fragmentation with clean DNA ladder`],[`Energy requirement`,`None, passive`,`ATP dependent, active`],[`Inflammation`,`Present`,`Absent`],[`Key mediators`,`Enzymatic digestion, free radicals`,`Caspases, Bcl-2 family, cytochrome c`],[`Fate of debris`,`Phagocytosed after inflammation`,`Apoptotic bodies engulfed by neighbours`]]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type of necrosis`,`Typical site`],rows:[[`Coagulative`,`Infarcts of heart, kidney, spleen`],[`Liquefactive`,`Brain infarct, abscess`],[`Caseous`,`Tuberculosis`],[`Fat necrosis`,`Acute pancreatitis, breast trauma`],[`Fibrinoid`,`Malignant hypertension, vasculitis`],[`Gangrenous`,`Lower limb, gut - coagulative plus infection`]],caption:`Brain is the exception: it undergoes liquefactive necrosis after infarction while every other organ undergoes coagulative necrosis.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Events of acute inflammation`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Transient vasoconstriction of arterioles lasting seconds.`,`Persistent vasodilatation - the cause of redness and heat, mediated by histamine, nitric oxide and prostaglandins.`,`Increased vascular permeability, chiefly by endothelial contraction in venules, giving a protein-rich exudate and swelling.`,`Slowing of blood flow (stasis) as haemoconcentration follows fluid loss.`,`Margination and rolling of leucocytes along the endothelium, mediated by selectins.`,`Firm adhesion through integrins binding ICAM-1 and VCAM-1.`,`Transmigration (diapedesis) between endothelial cells, guided by PECAM-1.`,`Chemotaxis towards C5a, leukotriene B4, IL-8 and bacterial peptides.`,`Phagocytosis - recognition and attachment, engulfment, killing by the respiratory burst and lysosomal enzymes.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Mediator`,`Source`,`Main action`],rows:[[`Histamine`,`Mast cells, basophils, platelets`,`Vasodilatation, venular permeability`],[`Serotonin`,`Platelets`,`Vasodilatation, permeability`],[`Prostaglandins`,`Membrane phospholipid via COX`,`Vasodilatation, pain, fever`],[`Leukotriene B4`,`Membrane phospholipid via LOX`,`Chemotaxis of neutrophils`],[`Leukotrienes C4, D4, E4`,`Mast cells`,`Bronchospasm, permeability`],[`Bradykinin`,`Kinin system from kininogen`,`Pain, vasodilatation, permeability`],[`C3a and C5a`,`Complement`,`Anaphylatoxins; C5a is chemotactic`],[`TNF-alpha and IL-1`,`Macrophages`,`Endothelial activation, fever, acute phase response`],[`IL-8`,`Macrophages`,`Neutrophil chemotaxis`],[`Nitric oxide`,`Endothelium, macrophages`,`Vasodilatation, microbicidal`],[`Platelet activating factor`,`Many leucocytes`,`Permeability, platelet aggregation`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Chronic inflammation and repair`},{kind:`para`,text:`A granuloma is a focus of chronic inflammation made of epithelioid cells - activated macrophages with abundant pink cytoplasm - surrounded by lymphocytes, often with multinucleate giant cells. Caseating granulomas occur in tuberculosis; non-caseating granulomas in sarcoidosis, leprosy, Crohn disease and foreign body reactions. Langhans giant cells have nuclei arranged in a horseshoe at the periphery.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Healing by first intention`,`Healing by second intention`],rows:[[`Wound`,`Clean, apposed surgical incision`,`Large wound with tissue loss`],[`Inflammation`,`Minimal`,`Intense`],[`Granulation tissue`,`Scanty`,`Abundant`],[`Wound contraction`,`Absent or trivial`,`Marked, by myofibroblasts`],[`Time to heal`,`Days`,`Weeks`],[`Scar`,`Thin, linear`,`Large, often disfiguring`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Exudate versus transudate`,text:`Exudate has specific gravity above 1.020 and protein above 3 g/dL because vessels are leaky; transudate has specific gravity below 1.012 and protein below 3 g/dL because only hydrostatic or oncotic pressure has changed. Inflammation gives exudate; heart failure and hypoalbuminaemia give transudate.`}]},{topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions turn on the molecular sequence of ischaemic injury, free radical chemistry, the intrinsic and extrinsic apoptosis pathways, the physics of oedema and the haemodynamics of shock.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Hypoxia stops oxidative phosphorylation, so ATP falls.`,`The Na+/K+ ATPase fails; sodium and water enter the cell and it swells, while potassium leaks out.`,`Anaerobic glycolysis takes over; glycogen is consumed, lactate accumulates and intracellular pH falls.`,`Ribosomes detach from the rough endoplasmic reticulum and protein synthesis declines.`,`Up to this point the changes are reversible.`,`Rising cytosolic calcium activates phospholipases, proteases, endonucleases and ATPases.`,`Mitochondrial permeability transition pores open, cytochrome c escapes and membranes are irreparably damaged - the point of no return.`,`Restoring blood flow can itself worsen damage: reperfusion injury from a burst of free radicals, calcium overload and complement activation.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The main reactive oxygen species are superoxide anion, hydrogen peroxide and the hydroxyl radical, the last being the most destructive; they cause lipid peroxidation, protein cross-linking and DNA strand breaks.`,`Antioxidant defences are superoxide dismutase, catalase, glutathione peroxidase, vitamins E and C, and metal binding proteins such as transferrin and ceruloplasmin.`,`Carbon tetrachloride is converted by CYP2E1 to the CCl3 radical; paracetamol overdose forms NAPQI once glutathione is depleted, which is why N-acetylcysteine is the antidote.`,`Free radical injury underlies chemical carcinogenesis, ageing, and reperfusion after myocardial infarction or stroke.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Apoptotic pathway`,`Trigger`,`Key molecules`],rows:[[`Intrinsic (mitochondrial)`,`DNA damage, growth factor withdrawal`,`Bax and Bak release cytochrome c, Apaf-1, caspase 9`],[`Extrinsic (death receptor)`,`Fas ligand, TNF binding`,`FADD, caspase 8`],[`Executioner phase`,`Both pathways converge`,`Caspases 3, 6 and 7; endonuclease DNA ladder`]],caption:`Bcl-2 and Bcl-xL are anti-apoptotic; their overexpression by the t(14;18) translocation causes follicular lymphoma. p53 promotes apoptosis, so its loss lets damaged cells survive.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Starling forces: Net filtration = Kf x [ (Pc - Pi) - sigma x (pi c - pi i) ]`,caption:`Pc capillary hydrostatic pressure, Pi interstitial hydrostatic pressure, pi c plasma oncotic pressure, pi i interstitial oncotic pressure, sigma the reflection coefficient. Oedema follows a rise in Pc, a fall in pi c, lymphatic obstruction or increased permeability.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Oedema mechanism`,`Clinical example`],rows:[[`Raised hydrostatic pressure`,`Congestive cardiac failure, venous thrombosis`],[`Reduced plasma oncotic pressure`,`Nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, kwashiorkor`],[`Lymphatic obstruction`,`Filariasis, post-mastectomy lymphoedema`],[`Increased vascular permeability`,`Inflammation, burns, anaphylaxis`],[`Sodium and water retention`,`Renal failure, excess aldosterone`]]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type of shock`,`Mechanism`,`Cardiac output`,`Peripheral resistance`],rows:[[`Hypovolaemic`,`Blood or fluid loss`,`Low`,`High`],[`Cardiogenic`,`Pump failure, e.g. massive MI`,`Low`,`High`],[`Septic`,`Endotoxin, cytokine storm, vasodilatation`,`High early, low late`,`Low`],[`Anaphylactic`,`Type I hypersensitivity, mediator release`,`Low`,`Low`],[`Neurogenic`,`Loss of vasomotor tone`,`Low`,`Low`]],caption:`Septic and anaphylactic shock are distributive - the volume is normal but the container is too large, so the skin stays warm early on.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Stages of shock: non-progressive or compensated, in which baroreceptor reflexes, catecholamines and RAAS maintain perfusion; progressive, with tissue hypoperfusion, lactic acidosis and arteriolar dilatation; irreversible, with cell and organ damage that persists even after resuscitation.`,`Labile cells (gut and skin epithelium, marrow) divide continuously and regenerate fully; stable cells (liver, kidney tubule, fibroblast) divide only when stimulated; permanent cells (neurons, cardiac and skeletal muscle) cannot regenerate and heal only by scar.`,`Wound strength reaches about 10 percent of normal at one week, and plateaus at about 70 to 80 percent after three months as type III collagen is replaced by type I.`,`Healing is delayed by infection, poor blood supply, foreign bodies, diabetes, corticosteroids, and deficiency of protein, vitamin C or zinc. Vitamin C is needed for hydroxylation of proline and lysine in collagen.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Repeatedly asked distinctions`,text:`Keloid extends beyond the wound margin and recurs after excision; a hypertrophic scar stays within the margin and regresses. Dystrophic calcification occurs in dead tissue with normal plasma calcium; metastatic calcification occurs in normal tissue with raised plasma calcium.`}]},{topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This topic covers what goes wrong in the heart, the blood vessels and the lungs. Most of it follows from one idea: if a tissue does not get enough blood, or if a tube that should be open is narrowed, the organ fails.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Blood vessels and the heart`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Atherosclerosis: fatty plaques build up in the wall of a large artery, narrowing it and making the surface rough so a clot can form on it.`,`Ischaemia: reduced blood supply to a tissue. If it is severe and prolonged, the tissue dies - an infarct.`,`Angina pectoris: chest pain on exertion because the coronary supply cannot meet demand. The muscle does not die.`,`Myocardial infarction: a coronary artery is blocked completely and part of the heart muscle dies.`,`Hypertension: blood pressure persistently at or above 140/90 mm Hg. It usually causes no symptoms but damages the heart, brain, kidneys and eyes over years.`,`Heart failure: the heart cannot pump enough blood for the body needs, so fluid backs up - breathlessness from the left side, swollen legs and liver from the right side.`,`Arrhythmia: an abnormal heart rhythm, too fast, too slow or irregular.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The lungs`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Asthma: reversible narrowing of the airways with wheeze, cough and breathlessness, usually triggered by an allergen and worse at night.`,`Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD): largely irreversible airway obstruction from long-term smoking; it covers chronic bronchitis and emphysema.`,`Pneumonia: infection and consolidation of the lung tissue with fever, cough and purulent sputum.`,`Tuberculosis: chronic infection with Mycobacterium tuberculosis, giving cough for weeks, evening fever, night sweats and weight loss.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Blood pressure is written as systolic over diastolic. Normal is below 120/80 mm Hg. Because raised pressure is silent, it is called the silent killer and is found only by measuring it.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Basic recall`,text:`The commonest cause of myocardial infarction is thrombosis on a ruptured atherosclerotic plaque; the commonest cause of right heart failure is left heart failure; about 90 to 95 percent of hypertension is essential, that is with no identified cause.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Left versus right failure`,text:`Left fails, Lungs flood (breathlessness, orthopnoea). Right fails, Rest of the body swells (ankle oedema, raised jugular pressure, tender liver).`}]},{topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level you need the pathogenesis of the atherosclerotic plaque, the blood pressure classification with target organ damage, the timing of cardiac markers after infarction, and the asthma versus COPD table.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Atherosclerosis`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Endothelial injury from hypertension, smoking, hyperlipidaemia, diabetes or homocysteine.`,`LDL enters the intima and is oxidised there.`,`Monocytes adhere, migrate in and become macrophages that engulf oxidised LDL through scavenger receptors, becoming foam cells - the fatty streak.`,`Platelets and activated cells release PDGF, FGF and TGF-beta.`,`Smooth muscle cells migrate from the media into the intima and lay down collagen, forming the fibrous cap over a lipid core - the fibrofatty atheroma.`,`Complications follow: calcification, ulceration, plaque rupture with superimposed thrombosis, haemorrhage into the plaque, and aneurysm of the weakened wall.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Non-modifiable risk factors: age, male sex, family history, genetics.`,`Modifiable risk factors: hyperlipidaemia with high LDL and low HDL, hypertension, smoking, diabetes, obesity, physical inactivity, stress.`,`A vulnerable plaque is one with a large lipid core, a thin fibrous cap and heavy inflammation; it ruptures despite causing little stenosis, which is why a mild-looking lesion can cause a fatal infarct.`,`Commonest sites in descending order: abdominal aorta, coronary arteries, popliteal artery, internal carotid, circle of Willis.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Hypertension`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Category`,`Systolic (mm Hg)`,`Diastolic (mm Hg)`],rows:[[`Normal`,`Below 120`,`Below 80`],[`Elevated / pre-hypertension`,`120 to 139`,`80 to 89`],[`Stage 1 hypertension`,`140 to 159`,`90 to 99`],[`Stage 2 hypertension`,`160 or above`,`100 or above`],[`Hypertensive crisis`,`Above 180`,`Above 120`]],caption:`Thresholds differ slightly between guidelines; the JNC-style 140/90 cut-off is the one usually examined.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Target organ`,`Damage produced`],rows:[[`Heart`,`Left ventricular hypertrophy, heart failure, ischaemic heart disease`],[`Brain`,`Stroke, hypertensive encephalopathy, Charcot-Bouchard aneurysm`],[`Kidney`,`Nephrosclerosis, proteinuria, chronic renal failure`],[`Eye`,`Hypertensive retinopathy, papilloedema in malignant phase`],[`Large vessels`,`Aortic dissection, aneurysm`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Secondary hypertension, about 5 to 10 percent of cases: renal artery stenosis, chronic kidney disease, phaeochromocytoma, Conn syndrome, Cushing syndrome, coarctation of the aorta, oral contraceptives, NSAIDs.`,`Malignant or accelerated hypertension is a rapid rise with diastolic above 120 mm Hg, papilloedema and fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles; it is a medical emergency.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Ischaemic heart disease`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Marker`,`Rises at`,`Peaks at`,`Returns to normal`],rows:[[`Myoglobin`,`1 to 2 hours`,`6 to 9 hours`,`24 hours`],[`CK-MB`,`3 to 6 hours`,`18 to 24 hours`,`48 to 72 hours`],[`Troponin I and T`,`3 to 6 hours`,`18 to 24 hours`,`7 to 10 days`],[`LDH (LDH1)`,`10 hours`,`48 to 72 hours`,`7 to 14 days`]],caption:`Troponin is the most sensitive and specific marker; CK-MB is preferred for detecting re-infarction because it falls back to normal quickly.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Stable angina: fixed stenosis, pain on exertion, relieved by rest and nitrates.`,`Unstable angina: plaque rupture with non-occlusive thrombus, pain at rest, no rise in troponin.`,`Prinzmetal or variant angina: coronary spasm, pain at rest, ST elevation that resolves.`,`Myocardial infarction: occlusive thrombus with myocyte necrosis and a rise in troponin. The left anterior descending artery is involved most often, in about 40 to 50 percent of cases.`,`Complications of MI: arrhythmia (commonest cause of early death), cardiogenic shock, cardiac failure, mural thrombus and embolism, ventricular rupture at 3 to 7 days, and Dressler syndrome after weeks.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Heart failure and arrhythmias`},{kind:`para`,text:`In congestive cardiac failure the falling cardiac output activates the sympathetic system and RAAS. These raise heart rate, contractility, preload and afterload, which helps briefly but then increases the work of a failing ventricle, causing ventricular remodelling and a vicious cycle. This is why ACE inhibitors, beta blockers and aldosterone antagonists improve survival while positive inotropes largely do not.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Left ventricular failure gives pulmonary congestion: dyspnoea, orthopnoea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, pink frothy sputum, heart failure cells (haemosiderin-laden macrophages) in the alveoli.`,`Right ventricular failure gives systemic congestion: raised jugular venous pressure, pitting pedal oedema, tender hepatomegaly with nutmeg liver, ascites.`,`Cor pulmonale is right heart failure caused by lung disease, most often COPD.`,`Arrhythmias arise from abnormal automaticity, triggered activity (early and delayed after-depolarisations) or re-entry. Atrial fibrillation is the commonest sustained arrhythmia and predisposes to embolic stroke; ventricular fibrillation is the usual mechanism of sudden cardiac death.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Asthma versus COPD`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Asthma`,`COPD`],rows:[[`Typical age of onset`,`Childhood or young adult`,`After 40 years`],[`Chief cause`,`Atopy, allergen exposure`,`Smoking`],[`Reversibility`,`Largely reversible`,`Largely irreversible`],[`Inflammatory cells`,`Eosinophils, mast cells, CD4 Th2`,`Neutrophils, macrophages, CD8`],[`Symptom pattern`,`Episodic, worse at night`,`Slowly progressive, daily`],[`Sputum findings`,`Curschmann spirals, Charcot-Leyden crystals`,`Mucopurulent, no eosinophils`],[`Response to steroids`,`Good`,`Limited`],[`Diffusing capacity`,`Normal or raised`,`Reduced in emphysema`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Chronic bronchitis versus emphysema`,text:`Chronic bronchitis is defined clinically - productive cough for 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years - and gives the blue bloater picture with cyanosis and oedema. Emphysema is defined anatomically as permanent dilatation of airspaces distal to the terminal bronchiole with wall destruction, and gives the pink puffer picture with dyspnoea and pursed lip breathing.`}]},{topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced layer is the time course of the infarct under the microscope, the pressure-volume consequences of failure, the alpha-1 antitrypsin story and the immunology of tuberculosis.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Time after infarction`,`Gross change`,`Microscopic change`],rows:[[`0 to 4 hours`,`None`,`None or wavy fibres`],[`4 to 12 hours`,`Mottling`,`Early coagulative necrosis, oedema`],[`1 to 3 days`,`Yellow pallor`,`Dense neutrophil infiltrate`],[`3 to 7 days`,`Hyperaemic border`,`Macrophages digest dead fibres - wall is weakest`],[`7 to 14 days`,`Depressed red-grey`,`Granulation tissue, new vessels`],[`2 to 8 weeks`,`Grey-white scar`,`Collagen deposition`],[`Over 2 months`,`Firm scar`,`Dense collagen, no cells`]],caption:`Free wall rupture, papillary muscle rupture and septal rupture cluster at 3 to 7 days, when macrophages have removed the necrotic muscle but collagen has not yet been laid down.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Systolic failure means impaired contractility with a reduced ejection fraction, typically below 40 percent, as after infarction or in dilated cardiomyopathy. Diastolic failure means impaired filling with a preserved ejection fraction, as in left ventricular hypertrophy, restrictive cardiomyopathy or constrictive pericarditis.`,`Concentric hypertrophy with sarcomeres added in parallel follows pressure overload such as hypertension or aortic stenosis; eccentric hypertrophy with sarcomeres added in series follows volume overload such as mitral or aortic regurgitation.`,`Brain natriuretic peptide released by the stretched ventricle is used to confirm or exclude heart failure; it promotes natriuresis and vasodilatation, opposing the RAAS.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Ejection fraction = Stroke volume / End diastolic volume x 100; normal 55 to 70 percent`,caption:`An EDV of 120 mL with an ESV of 50 mL gives SV 70 mL and EF 58 percent. Below 40 percent defines heart failure with reduced ejection fraction.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Centriacinar emphysema affects the respiratory bronchioles of the upper lobes and is the smoking-related pattern. Panacinar emphysema affects the whole acinus, is worst in the lower lobes and is the pattern of alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, whose PiZZ genotype also causes childhood liver disease.`,`Smoking causes emphysema by the protease-antiprotease imbalance: neutrophil elastase is increased and alpha-1 antitrypsin is inactivated by oxidants in smoke, so elastin is destroyed and alveolar walls are lost.`,`In asthma the early phase within minutes is mast cell degranulation with histamine and leukotrienes causing bronchospasm; the late phase at 4 to 8 hours is eosinophil and Th2 cell driven with mucosal oedema and mucus, which is why an inhaled corticosteroid is the controller drug.`,`Chronic asthma remodels the airway: basement membrane thickening, goblet cell hyperplasia, smooth muscle hypertrophy and submucosal gland enlargement.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Pneumonia pattern`,`Distribution`,`Usual organism`],rows:[[`Lobar pneumonia`,`Whole lobe, four classic stages`,`Streptococcus pneumoniae`],[`Bronchopneumonia`,`Patchy, around bronchioles`,`Staphylococcus, Klebsiella, Haemophilus`],[`Atypical / interstitial`,`Interstitium, little exudate`,`Mycoplasma, Chlamydia, viruses`],[`Aspiration pneumonia`,`Dependent segments, right lower lobe`,`Anaerobes, mixed flora`]],caption:`The four stages of lobar pneumonia are congestion, red hepatisation, grey hepatisation and resolution.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Tuberculosis is a type IV delayed hypersensitivity reaction. Mycobacteria survive inside macrophages by blocking phagolysosome fusion; CD4 Th1 cells then secrete interferon gamma, which activates macrophages into epithelioid and Langhans giant cells, forming the caseating granuloma or tubercle.`,`Primary tuberculosis produces the Ghon focus in the lower part of the upper lobe or the upper part of the lower lobe, plus hilar node involvement - together the Ghon complex. Secondary or post-primary disease is typically apical with cavitation, because oxygen tension there is highest.`,`Miliary tuberculosis is haematogenous spread giving countless millet-sized lesions; Pott disease is spinal tuberculosis; scrofula is cervical lymph node disease.`,`The tuberculin skin test measures sensitisation, not active disease, and may be falsely negative in miliary disease, HIV infection, sarcoidosis, measles and severe malnutrition - the state of anergy.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Facts that decide the mark`,text:`Virchow triad for thrombosis is endothelial injury, abnormal blood flow and hypercoagulability. Lines of Zahn indicate a thrombus formed in flowing blood, distinguishing it from a post-mortem clot. Subendocardial infarcts spare the outer wall because the subendocardium is the last region to be perfused and the first to suffer.`}]},{topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This topic covers three groups of disease: hormones secreted in too great or too small an amount, kidneys that stop filtering properly, and a liver or stomach that is damaged. Each is defined here in plain terms.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Endocrine disease`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Diabetes mellitus: blood glucose stays high because there is too little insulin or the body no longer responds to it. Symptoms are excessive thirst, excessive urination, excessive hunger and weight loss.`,`Hyperthyroidism: too much thyroid hormone, so metabolism runs fast - weight loss despite good appetite, fast pulse, heat intolerance, tremor.`,`Hypothyroidism: too little thyroid hormone - weight gain, cold intolerance, slow pulse, constipation, dry skin. In an infant it causes cretinism, in an adult myxoedema.`,`Cushing syndrome: too much cortisol - moon face, buffalo hump, central obesity with thin limbs, purple striae, high blood sugar and high blood pressure.`,`Addison disease: too little cortisol and aldosterone - tiredness, low blood pressure, low sodium, high potassium and darkening of the skin.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Kidney disease`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Acute renal failure: kidney function falls over hours to days. Urine output usually drops and waste products such as urea and creatinine accumulate. It is often reversible.`,`Chronic renal failure: function is lost slowly over months to years and does not return. The end stage needs dialysis or transplant.`,`Nephrotic syndrome: the filter leaks large amounts of protein, so the patient loses albumin in urine and swells all over.`,`Nephritic syndrome: the filter is inflamed, so blood and some protein appear in urine with high blood pressure and reduced urine output.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Liver and stomach`},{kind:`para`,text:`Jaundice is yellow discolouration of the skin and eyes caused by too much bilirubin, the yellow pigment made when red cells break down. Hepatitis is inflammation of the liver, usually viral. Cirrhosis is the end stage of long-term liver damage, in which normal liver is replaced by nodules of regenerating cells separated by scar tissue. A peptic ulcer is a break in the lining of the stomach or duodenum deep enough to reach the muscle layer.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Recall facts`,text:`Normal fasting plasma glucose is below 100 mg/dL; diabetes is diagnosed at a fasting value of 126 mg/dL or more, a 2-hour post-glucose value of 200 mg/dL or more, or HbA1c of 6.5 percent or more. Normal serum bilirubin is up to 1 mg/dL and jaundice becomes visible above about 2 to 3 mg/dL.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Cushing versus Addison`,text:`CUSHing - Cortisol Up, Sugar High. Addison - Adds pigment, subtracts everything else (low BP, low sodium, low cortisol).`}]},{topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core here is the type 1 versus type 2 diabetes table with its complications, the nephrotic versus nephritic comparison, the three-column bilirubin pattern of jaundice, and the pathogenesis of peptic ulcer.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Diabetes mellitus`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Type 1`,`Type 2`],rows:[[`Share of cases`,`About 5 to 10 percent`,`About 90 percent`],[`Usual age at onset`,`Below 30 years`,`Above 40 years`],[`Body build`,`Lean`,`Usually obese`],[`Basic defect`,`Autoimmune beta cell destruction`,`Insulin resistance with relative deficiency`],[`Islet antibodies`,`Present (anti-GAD, anti-islet cell)`,`Absent`],[`HLA association`,`HLA-DR3 and DR4`,`None`],[`Twin concordance`,`About 50 percent`,`Nearly 90 percent`],[`Plasma insulin`,`Very low or absent`,`Normal, high or low`],[`Ketoacidosis`,`Common`,`Rare; hyperosmolar coma instead`],[`Treatment`,`Insulin essential`,`Diet, oral agents, later insulin`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Acute complications: diabetic ketoacidosis with hyperglycaemia, ketosis and high anion gap metabolic acidosis, mainly in type 1; hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state with glucose often above 600 mg/dL and no significant ketosis, mainly in type 2; and hypoglycaemia from treatment.`,`Chronic microvascular complications: retinopathy, nephropathy and neuropathy. These follow the polyol pathway, non-enzymatic glycation with advanced glycation end products, and protein kinase C activation.`,`Chronic macrovascular complications: accelerated atherosclerosis giving ischaemic heart disease, stroke and peripheral vascular disease. Cardiovascular disease is the commonest cause of death in diabetes.`,`Diabetic nephropathy passes from hyperfiltration to microalbuminuria (30 to 300 mg/day) to overt proteinuria and then renal failure; the classical lesion is Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodular glomerulosclerosis.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Thyroid and adrenal`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Disorder`,`T3 and T4`,`TSH`,`Typical cause`],rows:[[`Graves disease`,`High`,`Low`,`TSH receptor stimulating IgG antibody`],[`Primary hypothyroidism`,`Low`,`High`,`Hashimoto thyroiditis, iodine deficiency`],[`Secondary hypothyroidism`,`Low`,`Low or normal`,`Pituitary failure`],[`Subclinical hypothyroidism`,`Normal`,`High`,`Early thyroid failure`]],caption:`Graves disease adds exophthalmos and pretibial myxoedema, which are not seen with other causes of thyrotoxicosis.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Cushing syndrome`,`Addison disease`],rows:[[`Cortisol`,`Excess`,`Deficient`],[`Commonest cause`,`Exogenous steroid therapy`,`Autoimmune adrenalitis; TB worldwide`],[`Blood pressure`,`Raised`,`Low, with postural drop`],[`Sodium and potassium`,`Na+ high, K+ low`,`Na+ low, K+ high`],[`Blood glucose`,`High`,`Low`],[`Skin`,`Purple striae, thin skin, bruising`,`Hyperpigmentation of creases and mucosa`],[`Screening test`,`Overnight dexamethasone suppression`,`Short ACTH (Synacthen) stimulation`]],caption:`Pigmentation occurs in primary adrenal failure because low cortisol releases POMC-derived ACTH and MSH from feedback inhibition; it is absent in secondary (pituitary) failure.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Renal failure and glomerular syndromes`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Acute renal failure`,`Chronic renal failure`],rows:[[`Onset`,`Hours to days`,`Months to years`],[`Kidney size on ultrasound`,`Normal or enlarged`,`Small and shrunken`],[`Anaemia`,`Usually absent early`,`Present, normocytic`],[`Bone disease`,`Absent`,`Renal osteodystrophy`],[`Reversibility`,`Often reversible`,`Irreversible`],[`Causes`,`Prerenal, renal (ATN), postrenal`,`Diabetes, hypertension, glomerulonephritis`]]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Nephrotic syndrome`,`Nephritic syndrome`],rows:[[`Proteinuria`,`Heavy, above 3.5 g/day`,`Mild to moderate, below 3.5 g/day`],[`Haematuria`,`Absent or trivial`,`Present, with red cell casts`],[`Oedema`,`Marked, generalised, periorbital`,`Mild, chiefly facial`],[`Blood pressure`,`Usually normal`,`Raised`],[`Serum albumin`,`Low`,`Normal or slightly low`],[`Lipids`,`Hyperlipidaemia, lipiduria`,`Normal`],[`Typical lesion`,`Minimal change, membranous`,`Post-streptococcal, IgA nephropathy`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Jaundice, hepatitis and peptic ulcer`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Unconjugated`,`Conjugated`,`Urine bilirubin`,`Urobilinogen`,`Stool`],rows:[[`Prehepatic (haemolytic)`,`Markedly raised`,`Normal`,`Absent`,`Increased`,`Dark`],[`Hepatic (hepatocellular)`,`Raised`,`Raised`,`Present`,`Normal or reduced`,`Normal or pale`],[`Posthepatic (obstructive)`,`Normal`,`Markedly raised`,`Present, dark urine`,`Absent`,`Clay coloured`]],caption:`Only conjugated bilirubin is water soluble, so bilirubin appears in urine only when the conjugated fraction rises - hence the absence of bilirubinuria in haemolytic jaundice, called acholuric jaundice.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Peptic ulcer results from an imbalance between aggressive factors - acid, pepsin, Helicobacter pylori, NSAIDs, smoking, bile reflux - and defensive factors - mucus, bicarbonate, mucosal blood flow, prostaglandins and rapid epithelial regeneration. H. pylori is found in about 90 percent of duodenal ulcers and 70 percent of gastric ulcers. It survives the acid by splitting urea with urease to produce ammonia, damages epithelium with cytotoxin CagA and VacA, and induces chronic antral gastritis with raised gastrin.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Gastric versus duodenal ulcer`,text:`Duodenal ulcer pain is relieved by food and typically wakes the patient at night, with normal or high acid output; gastric ulcer pain is worsened by food, with normal or low acid output and a defective mucosal barrier. Malignancy must be excluded in a gastric ulcer, but duodenal ulcers essentially never turn malignant.`}]},{topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced marks come from the biochemistry of chronic hyperglycaemia, the causes and staging of renal failure, the serology of viral hepatitis and the mechanism of ascites in cirrhosis.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Polyol pathway: excess glucose is reduced by aldose reductase to sorbitol using NADPH. Sorbitol accumulates osmotically in lens, nerve and retina, while NADPH depletion lowers glutathione and raises oxidative stress - the basis of diabetic cataract and neuropathy.`,`Non-enzymatic glycation of proteins gives advanced glycation end products that cross-link collagen, trap LDL in vessel walls and bind RAGE receptors to trigger inflammation. HbA1c is glycated haemoglobin and reflects the mean glucose over the preceding 8 to 12 weeks, the lifespan of the red cell.`,`Insulin resistance in type 2 diabetes involves free fatty acids and adipokines: raised TNF-alpha and resistin and reduced adiponectin impair insulin receptor signalling through IRS-1 serine phosphorylation. Amylin deposits as islet amyloid in long-standing disease.`,`In ketoacidosis, unopposed glucagon drives lipolysis and hepatic ketogenesis; acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate produce a high anion gap acidosis with Kussmaul breathing, and total body potassium is depleted even though the measured serum potassium may be normal or high.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Anion gap = Na+ - (Cl- + HCO3-); normal 8 to 12 mEq/L`,caption:`With Na+ 135, Cl- 95 and HCO3- 10, the gap is 30 mEq/L, a high anion gap acidosis consistent with ketoacidosis. Causes are remembered as MUDPILES - methanol, uraemia, diabetic ketoacidosis, propylene glycol, isoniazid, lactic acidosis, ethylene glycol, salicylate.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Category of acute renal failure`,`Urine Na+`,`Urine osmolality`,`Typical cause`],rows:[[`Prerenal`,`Below 20 mEq/L`,`Above 500 mOsm/kg`,`Hypovolaemia, shock, heart failure`],[`Intrinsic (acute tubular necrosis)`,`Above 40 mEq/L`,`Below 350 mOsm/kg`,`Ischaemia, aminoglycosides, contrast`],[`Postrenal`,`Variable`,`Variable`,`Stones, prostatic enlargement, tumour`]],caption:`In prerenal failure the tubules are intact and avidly conserve sodium and water; in tubular necrosis they cannot, so urine is dilute and sodium rich.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Uraemia produces anaemia from loss of erythropoietin, bone disease from failure of 1-alpha-hydroxylation with secondary hyperparathyroidism, metabolic acidosis, hyperkalaemia, hypertension, pericarditis, encephalopathy and platelet dysfunction with bleeding.`,`Nephrotic syndrome carries a hypercoagulable state from urinary loss of antithrombin III, increased infection risk from loss of immunoglobulin, and hyperlipidaemia from increased hepatic lipoprotein synthesis in response to low oncotic pressure.`,`Minimal change disease shows a normal glomerulus on light microscopy with fusion of podocyte foot processes on electron microscopy; it is the commonest nephrotic syndrome of children and responds well to corticosteroids.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Virus`,`Genome`,`Transmission`,`Chronicity`],rows:[[`Hepatitis A`,`RNA`,`Faeco-oral`,`Never chronic`],[`Hepatitis B`,`DNA`,`Blood, sexual, vertical`,`About 5 to 10 percent in adults`],[`Hepatitis C`,`RNA`,`Blood, mainly parenteral`,`About 80 percent`],[`Hepatitis D`,`RNA (defective)`,`Blood; needs HBV coat`,`With HBV superinfection`],[`Hepatitis E`,`RNA`,`Faeco-oral, water borne`,`Rarely; severe in pregnancy`]],caption:`HBsAg persisting beyond six months defines chronic hepatitis B; anti-HBs alone indicates immunity from vaccination, while IgM anti-HBc identifies acute infection and covers the window period when HBsAg has gone and anti-HBs has not yet appeared.`},{kind:`para`,text:`In cirrhosis, fibrous septa and regenerative nodules obstruct sinusoidal flow and raise portal pressure above the normal 5 to 10 mm Hg. Ascites then arises from a combination of portal hypertension, low plasma albumin, increased hepatic lymph formation and secondary hyperaldosteronism from reduced hepatic clearance and effective hypovolaemia. Other consequences are oesophageal varices, splenomegaly, caput medusae, hepatic encephalopathy from failure to convert ammonia to urea, and hepatorenal syndrome.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Sharp distinctions`,text:`Kernicterus occurs only with unconjugated bilirubin because it is lipid soluble and crosses the immature blood-brain barrier. Gilbert syndrome is mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia from reduced UDP-glucuronyl transferase; Crigler-Najjar type I is near total absence of the enzyme; Dubin-Johnson is conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with a black pigmented liver.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Zollinger-Ellison syndrome`,text:`Recurrent, multiple ulcers in unusual sites such as the jejunum, with diarrhoea and very high acid output, point to a gastrin-secreting tumour rather than ordinary peptic ulcer disease. Here gastrin is high with high acid, whereas in pernicious anaemia gastrin is high because acid is absent.`}]},{topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A neoplasm or tumour is a mass of cells that grows more than it should and keeps growing even after the stimulus that started it has gone. Some tumours stay where they are; others invade and spread. This level also defines the four infections examined most often.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Benign and malignant`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Benign tumour: grows slowly, stays in one place, has a capsule around it, and does not spread. It is usually cured by removing it.`,`Malignant tumour, or cancer: grows fast, invades surrounding tissue, has no capsule, and can spread to distant organs.`,`Metastasis: the spread of cancer cells from the original site to a new site. It is the single feature that proves a tumour is malignant.`,`Carcinoma: a malignant tumour arising from epithelium, such as skin, lung, breast or gut lining.`,`Sarcoma: a malignant tumour arising from connective tissue such as bone, muscle or fat.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Tumours are named by their tissue of origin plus a suffix. The suffix -oma usually means benign, as in adenoma from gland or lipoma from fat. The suffixes -carcinoma and -sarcoma mean malignant. A few names break the rule: lymphoma, melanoma and myeloma are all malignant despite ending in -oma.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Four infections to know`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Malaria: caused by Plasmodium parasites carried by the female Anopheles mosquito. The parasite multiplies inside red cells and bursts them, giving cycles of fever with chills and rigors, anaemia and an enlarged spleen.`,`Typhoid: caused by Salmonella typhi from contaminated food or water. It gives a fever that climbs in a step-ladder pattern over the first week, abdominal pain and a slow pulse relative to the fever.`,`HIV and AIDS: the human immunodeficiency virus destroys CD4 helper T lymphocytes, so the immune system slowly fails and unusual infections and tumours appear.`,`Viral hepatitis: viruses A to E inflame the liver, causing jaundice, dark urine, loss of appetite and tenderness over the liver.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Straight recall`,text:`Plasmodium falciparum causes the most severe malaria including cerebral malaria; P. vivax and P. ovale have a dormant liver stage (hypnozoite) that causes relapses; HIV is a retrovirus and AIDS is diagnosed when the CD4 count falls below 200 cells per microlitre or an AIDS-defining illness appears.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`-omas that are malignant`,text:`Remember the liars: LyMphoma, Melanoma, Myeloma, Seminoma - all end in -oma but all are malignant.`}]},{topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core is the benign versus malignant table, the stages of carcinogenesis, the classes of cancer genes, the routes of metastasis, tumour markers, and the life cycle detail of malaria, typhoid and HIV.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Comparing benign and malignant tumours`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Benign`,`Malignant`],rows:[[`Rate of growth`,`Slow`,`Rapid`],[`Border`,`Encapsulated, well circumscribed`,`Infiltrating, no capsule`],[`Differentiation`,`Well differentiated`,`Poor, up to anaplastic`],[`Mitoses`,`Few, normal figures`,`Many, abnormal figures`],[`Nuclear features`,`Normal N:C ratio`,`High N:C ratio, pleomorphism, hyperchromasia`],[`Necrosis and haemorrhage`,`Rare`,`Common`],[`Metastasis`,`Never`,`Frequent`],[`Recurrence after excision`,`Rare`,`Common`],[`Effect on host`,`Usually local pressure`,`Cachexia, often fatal`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Carcinogenesis`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Initiation - a carcinogen produces a permanent, heritable DNA mutation in a single cell. It is rapid, irreversible and additive.`,`Promotion - repeated exposure to a promoter causes the initiated clone to proliferate. This step is slow and reversible, and a promoter alone cannot cause cancer.`,`Progression - further mutations accumulate, giving invasion, angiogenesis and metastatic capacity.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Gene class`,`Normal role`,`Effect of change`,`Example`],rows:[[`Proto-oncogene`,`Promotes normal growth`,`Gain of function, dominant`,`RAS, MYC, HER2/neu, ABL`],[`Tumour suppressor gene`,`Brakes the cell cycle`,`Loss of function, needs both alleles`,`p53, RB, APC, BRCA1 and 2`],[`Apoptosis regulator`,`Controls cell death`,`Excess survival signal`,`BCL-2 in follicular lymphoma`],[`DNA repair gene`,`Corrects replication errors`,`Genomic instability`,`MLH1, MSH2 in HNPCC`]],caption:`p53, the guardian of the genome, is the single most commonly mutated gene in human cancer; RB was the first tumour suppressor described and led to the Knudson two-hit hypothesis.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Carcinogen`,`Type`,`Tumour produced`],rows:[[`Benzpyrene (tobacco smoke)`,`Chemical`,`Lung, bladder carcinoma`],[`Aflatoxin B1`,`Chemical (fungal)`,`Hepatocellular carcinoma`],[`Vinyl chloride`,`Chemical`,`Angiosarcoma of liver`],[`Aromatic amines, beta-naphthylamine`,`Chemical`,`Bladder carcinoma`],[`Ultraviolet light`,`Physical`,`Skin cancers, melanoma`],[`Ionising radiation`,`Physical`,`Leukaemia, thyroid carcinoma`],[`Human papillomavirus 16 and 18`,`Viral`,`Cervical carcinoma`],[`Hepatitis B and C virus`,`Viral`,`Hepatocellular carcinoma`],[`Epstein-Barr virus`,`Viral`,`Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma`],[`Helicobacter pylori`,`Bacterial`,`Gastric carcinoma and MALT lymphoma`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Metastasis and markers`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Lymphatic spread is the usual route for carcinomas, reaching regional draining lymph nodes first.`,`Haematogenous spread is the usual route for sarcomas, favouring liver and lung because these receive portal and systemic venous drainage.`,`Transcoelomic spread across body cavities gives peritoneal seeding, as in ovarian carcinoma and the Krukenberg tumour of the ovary from a gastric primary.`,`Direct or contiguous spread invades adjacent structures, and perineural spread is characteristic of adenoid cystic carcinoma and prostate cancer.`,`The steps of metastasis are loss of E-cadherin adhesion, degradation of basement membrane by matrix metalloproteinases, migration, intravasation, survival in circulation, arrest and extravasation, and growth at the new site with angiogenesis driven by VEGF.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Marker`,`Associated tumour`],rows:[[`Alpha-fetoprotein`,`Hepatocellular carcinoma, yolk sac tumour`],[`Carcinoembryonic antigen`,`Colorectal, pancreatic, gastric carcinoma`],[`Prostate specific antigen`,`Carcinoma of prostate`],[`CA-125`,`Ovarian carcinoma`],[`CA 19-9`,`Pancreatic carcinoma`],[`Beta-hCG`,`Choriocarcinoma, hydatidiform mole, germ cell tumour`],[`Calcitonin`,`Medullary carcinoma of thyroid`],[`Bence Jones protein`,`Multiple myeloma`]],caption:`Tumour markers are used mainly to monitor treatment and detect recurrence; few are specific enough for screening, with PSA and AFP in high-risk groups the partial exceptions.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Infectious disease pathophysiology`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Malaria: sporozoites from the mosquito enter hepatocytes (exo-erythrocytic schizogony), then merozoites invade red cells. Rupture of infected red cells releases merozoites and pyrogens, producing the paroxysm of chills, fever and sweating. Fever periodicity is 48 hours in vivax and ovale (tertian), 72 hours in malariae (quartan) and irregular in falciparum. Falciparum infects red cells of all ages and causes cytoadherence and rosetting in capillaries, hence cerebral malaria, blackwater fever and severe anaemia.`,`Typhoid: ingested Salmonella typhi invades ileal Peyer patches, multiplies in macrophages and mesenteric nodes, then enters blood - the first bacteraemia. It seeds liver, spleen and gall bladder and re-enters blood in the second week. Peyer patches become hyperplastic then ulcerate longitudinally, so intestinal haemorrhage and perforation occur in the third week. Blood culture is positive in week 1, stool and urine cultures from week 2 to 3, and the Widal test becomes positive only from the end of week 1.`,`HIV: gp120 binds CD4 with CCR5 or CXCR4 as co-receptor; reverse transcriptase makes proviral DNA which integrates by integrase. Progressive CD4 depletion inverts the CD4 to CD8 ratio. Below 200 CD4 cells per microlitre, opportunistic infections such as Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia, oesophageal candidiasis, cryptococcal meningitis and toxoplasmosis appear, along with Kaposi sarcoma and lymphoma.`,`Viral hepatitis: hepatocyte injury is largely immune mediated by cytotoxic T cells rather than by direct viral cytopathic effect, which is why the clinical illness coincides with the immune response and why an immunosuppressed patient may have high viral load with mild hepatitis.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Grading is not staging`,text:`Grade describes how the tumour looks - the degree of differentiation and mitotic activity under the microscope. Stage describes how far it has spread, using the TNM system of tumour size, node involvement and metastasis. Stage is the stronger predictor of survival.`}]},{topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced work covers the hallmarks of cancer, named translocations, paraneoplastic syndromes, the timeline of HIV infection and drug-relevant details of these infections.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Hallmarks of cancer: self-sufficiency in growth signals, insensitivity to growth inhibition, evasion of apoptosis, limitless replicative potential through telomerase, sustained angiogenesis, invasion and metastasis, reprogrammed energy metabolism and evasion of immune destruction.`,`The Warburg effect is the preference of tumour cells for aerobic glycolysis even when oxygen is plentiful; it supplies carbon skeletons for biosynthesis and is the basis of FDG-PET imaging.`,`Telomerase reactivation lets tumour cells bypass replicative senescence; it is expressed in more than 85 percent of cancers but is silent in most somatic cells.`,`Angiogenesis is switched on when VEGF and FGF outweigh inhibitors such as thrombospondin-1 and angiostatin; a tumour cannot grow beyond about 1 to 2 mm without it.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Translocation`,`Fusion or gene`,`Malignancy`],rows:[[`t(9;22) Philadelphia`,`BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase`,`Chronic myeloid leukaemia`],[`t(8;14)`,`MYC to IgH promoter`,`Burkitt lymphoma`],[`t(14;18)`,`BCL-2 overexpression`,`Follicular lymphoma`],[`t(15;17)`,`PML-RAR alpha`,`Acute promyelocytic leukaemia`],[`HER2/neu amplification`,`ERBB2`,`Breast carcinoma`]],caption:`These are the classic drug-target examples: imatinib for BCR-ABL, all-trans retinoic acid for PML-RAR alpha, trastuzumab for HER2.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Paraneoplastic syndrome`,`Mediator`,`Usual tumour`],rows:[[`SIADH with hyponatraemia`,`ADH`,`Small cell carcinoma of lung`],[`Cushing syndrome`,`Ectopic ACTH`,`Small cell carcinoma of lung`],[`Hypercalcaemia`,`PTH-related peptide`,`Squamous cell carcinoma of lung`],[`Polycythaemia`,`Erythropoietin`,`Renal cell carcinoma`],[`Hypoglycaemia`,`Insulin-like growth factor 2`,`Hepatocellular carcinoma, sarcoma`]],caption:`Hypercalcaemia of malignancy may also be caused by osteolytic bone metastases; distinguishing the humoral from the local mechanism changes management.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Acute retroviral syndrome at 3 to 6 weeks after HIV exposure: fever, sore throat, rash and lymphadenopathy, with a burst of high viraemia and a transient CD4 fall.`,`Seroconversion at about 3 to 12 weeks; the interval before antibody is detectable is the window period, during which antigen or nucleic acid testing is needed.`,`Clinical latency lasting years, with viral replication continuing in lymphoid tissue and a steady decline in CD4 count of roughly 50 to 100 cells per microlitre per year.`,`Symptomatic phase below about 500 CD4 cells: oral candidiasis, hairy leucoplakia, herpes zoster, tuberculosis.`,`AIDS below 200 CD4 cells: Pneumocystis pneumonia, cryptococcal meningitis, cytomegalovirus retinitis, Mycobacterium avium complex, Kaposi sarcoma.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Falciparum malaria damages tissue by sequestration of parasitised red cells expressing PfEMP-1 on the endothelium of cerebral and other capillaries, so peripheral parasitaemia can underestimate the true burden. Hypoglycaemia, lactic acidosis and pulmonary oedema mark severe disease.`,`Only vivax and ovale form hypnozoites, so only they need primaquine for radical cure - and primaquine causes haemolysis in G6PD deficiency, which must be checked first.`,`Chronic carriage of Salmonella typhi persists in the gall bladder, often with gallstones, and is the state that produced the classic case of Typhoid Mary. Antibiotics reduce mortality but do not shorten carriage reliably; cholecystectomy may be needed.`,`Chronic hepatitis B and C cause hepatocellular carcinoma through repeated cycles of necrosis and regeneration plus, for HBV, integration of viral DNA and the HBx transactivator. Cirrhosis of any cause raises the risk, which is why alpha-fetoprotein and ultrasound are used for surveillance.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Facts examiners return to`,text:`Knudson two-hit hypothesis: a tumour suppressor needs both alleles inactivated, so an inherited first hit explains why familial retinoblastoma is early and bilateral while the sporadic form is late and unilateral. Carcinoma in situ shows full-thickness dysplasia with an intact basement membrane and is by definition not yet invasive.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Hamartoma, choristoma, teratoma`,text:`A hamartoma is a disorganised mass of tissue native to that site; a choristoma is normal tissue in an abnormal site; a teratoma is a true neoplasm from germ cells containing tissue from all three germ layers. Only the teratoma is a genuine neoplasm.`}]}],t=[{id:`q-pf-cell-injury-inflammation-1`,topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The first cell to arrive at the site of acute inflammation is the`,options:[`Neutrophil`,`Macrophage`,`Plasma cell`,`Lymphocyte`],answer:0,explanation:`Neutrophils dominate the first 6 to 24 hours of acute inflammation, arriving by margination, adhesion and diapedesis. Macrophages take over from about 24 to 48 hours and are the chief cell of chronic inflammation, which is the usual point of confusion.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pf-cell-injury-inflammation-2`,topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Increase in the size of the left ventricle in a patient with long-standing hypertension is an example of`,options:[`Dysplasia`,`Hyperplasia`,`Hypertrophy`,`Metaplasia`],answer:2,explanation:`Cardiac myocytes are permanent cells that cannot divide, so they respond to pressure overload by enlarging - hypertrophy. Hyperplasia would require an increase in cell number, which cardiac muscle is incapable of.`},{id:`q-pf-cell-injury-inflammation-3`,topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which type of necrosis characteristically occurs after infarction of the brain?`,options:[`Coagulative`,`Liquefactive`,`Caseous`,`Fibrinoid`],answer:1,explanation:`The brain is the exception to the rule: it is rich in lipid and hydrolytic enzymes and poor in structural protein, so an infarct liquefies into a cystic cavity. Every other organ, including heart, kidney and spleen, undergoes coagulative necrosis after infarction.`},{id:`q-pf-cell-injury-inflammation-4`,topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following is a feature of apoptosis but NOT of necrosis?`,options:[`Surrounding acute inflammation`,`Involvement of groups of adjacent cells`,`Cell swelling`,`Intact plasma membrane with formation of apoptotic bodies`],answer:3,explanation:`Apoptosis is an active, ATP-dependent process in which the cell shrinks and buds off membrane-bound apoptotic bodies, so contents never leak and no inflammation follows. Necrosis is the opposite on every count - swelling, membrane rupture, leakage and inflammation.`},{id:`q-pf-cell-injury-inflammation-5`,topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The most potent chemotactic complement fragment for neutrophils is`,options:[`C1q`,`C3b`,`C5a`,`C9`],answer:2,explanation:`C5a is both an anaphylatoxin and a powerful neutrophil chemoattractant, alongside leukotriene B4 and IL-8. C3b is the tempting distractor, but its role is opsonisation, coating microbes for phagocytosis rather than attracting cells.`},{id:`q-pf-cell-injury-inflammation-6`,topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A granuloma is composed principally of`,options:[`Epithelioid cells with lymphocytes and giant cells`,`Plasma cells and eosinophils`,`Proliferating capillaries and fibroblasts`,`Neutrophils and fibrin`],answer:0,explanation:`A granuloma is a collection of activated macrophages transformed into epithelioid cells, ringed by lymphocytes and often containing multinucleate giant cells. Proliferating capillaries with fibroblasts describes granulation tissue, a completely different entity despite the similar name.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pf-cell-injury-inflammation-7`,topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient with nephrotic syndrome develops generalised oedema. The chief mechanism is`,options:[`Lymphatic obstruction`,`Increased vascular permeability`,`Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure`,`Reduced plasma oncotic pressure from urinary albumin loss`],answer:3,explanation:`Loss of more than 3.5 g of protein a day lowers serum albumin and therefore plasma oncotic pressure, so filtration exceeds reabsorption at the capillary and fluid accumulates. Raised hydrostatic pressure is the mechanism in cardiac failure, and increased permeability is the mechanism in inflammation.`},{id:`q-pf-cell-injury-inflammation-8`,topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In septic shock, the peripheral vascular resistance and cardiac output in the early warm phase are respectively`,options:[`Increased and decreased`,`Decreased and increased`,`Increased and increased`,`Decreased and decreased`],answer:1,explanation:`Endotoxin-driven nitric oxide and cytokine release cause widespread vasodilatation, so resistance falls while the heart compensates with a high output - the warm, hyperdynamic phase. Hypovolaemic and cardiogenic shock show the opposite pattern of low output with reflex vasoconstriction.`},{id:`q-pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders-1`,topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The earliest visible lesion of atherosclerosis is the`,options:[`Fatty streak`,`Calcified nodule`,`Aneurysm`,`Fibrous plaque`],answer:0,explanation:`The fatty streak, a flat yellow intimal lesion made of lipid-laden foam cells, is the earliest change and may appear even in childhood. The fibrous plaque is the later, raised lesion in which smooth muscle and collagen form a cap over a lipid core.`},{id:`q-pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders-2`,topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Chronic bronchitis is defined clinically as a productive cough for`,options:[`Any duration if sputum is purulent`,`At least 1 month in a year`,`At least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years`,`At least 6 months continuously`],answer:2,explanation:`The definition is a productive cough for at least 3 months in each of 2 successive years with other causes excluded, and it is entirely clinical. Emphysema, by contrast, is defined anatomically by permanent airspace enlargement distal to the terminal bronchiole.`},{id:`q-pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders-3`,topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The most sensitive and specific serum marker for myocardial infarction is`,options:[`Lactate dehydrogenase`,`Cardiac troponin I`,`Myoglobin`,`Aspartate aminotransferase`],answer:1,explanation:`Cardiac troponin I and T are structural proteins found essentially only in myocardium, rise within 3 to 6 hours and remain raised for 7 to 10 days. Myoglobin rises earliest, within 1 to 2 hours, but is present in skeletal muscle too and so lacks specificity.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders-4`,topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which marker is preferred for detecting a re-infarction occurring 4 days after the initial myocardial infarction?`,options:[`LDH-1`,`C-reactive protein`,`Troponin T`,`CK-MB`],answer:3,explanation:`CK-MB returns to baseline within 48 to 72 hours, so a fresh rise at day 4 clearly signals new necrosis. Troponin is still elevated from the first event at that time, so it cannot distinguish new injury from the original infarct.`},{id:`q-pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders-5`,topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Curschmann spirals and Charcot-Leyden crystals in sputum are characteristic of`,options:[`Lobar pneumonia`,`Pulmonary tuberculosis`,`Bronchial asthma`,`Chronic bronchitis`],answer:2,explanation:`Curschmann spirals are twisted mucus casts of small airways and Charcot-Leyden crystals are formed from eosinophil membrane protein, so both point to eosinophilic asthma. COPD sputum is neutrophil rich and shows neither finding.`},{id:`q-pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders-6`,topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Heart failure cells seen in the alveoli in left ventricular failure are`,options:[`Haemosiderin-laden macrophages`,`Type II pneumocytes undergoing hyperplasia`,`Multinucleate giant cells`,`Neutrophils containing bacteria`],answer:0,explanation:`Chronic pulmonary venous congestion causes small haemorrhages, and macrophages that engulf the extravasated red cells become laden with haemosiderin - the heart failure cell. They indicate long-standing left-sided congestion, not infection.`},{id:`q-pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders-7`,topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Rupture of the ventricular free wall after myocardial infarction is most likely to occur at`,options:[`2 to 3 weeks`,`After 2 months`,`Within 6 hours`,`3 to 7 days`],answer:3,explanation:`By days 3 to 7 macrophages have digested the necrotic myocytes but granulation tissue has not yet laid down collagen, so the wall is at its mechanically weakest. After 2 weeks collagen deposition progressively strengthens the scar, and rupture becomes unlikely.`},{id:`q-pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders-8`,topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Panacinar emphysema affecting mainly the lower lobes of a young non-smoker suggests`,options:[`Primary ciliary dyskinesia`,`Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency`,`Chronic asthma`,`Occupational silicosis`],answer:1,explanation:`Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, classically the PiZZ genotype, removes the chief inhibitor of neutrophil elastase and produces panacinar, lower-lobe emphysema at a young age, often with liver disease. Smoking-related emphysema is centriacinar and upper-lobe predominant, the opposite pattern.`},{id:`q-pf-endocrine-renal-disorders-1`,topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Type 1 diabetes mellitus is characterised by`,options:[`Autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells`,`Excess glucagon secretion from alpha cells`,`Deficiency of insulin receptors only`,`Insulin resistance with normal beta cell mass`],answer:0,explanation:`Type 1 diabetes results from T cell mediated autoimmune destruction of beta cells, with anti-GAD and islet cell antibodies and an association with HLA-DR3 and DR4. Insulin resistance with preserved beta cell mass is the mechanism of type 2, which is the standard confusion.`},{id:`q-pf-endocrine-renal-disorders-2`,topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Clay coloured stools with dark urine and marked conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia indicate`,options:[`Haemolytic jaundice`,`Hepatocellular jaundice`,`Obstructive jaundice`,`Gilbert syndrome`],answer:2,explanation:`Biliary obstruction stops conjugated bilirubin reaching the gut, so stools lose their pigment while the water-soluble conjugated fraction spills into urine and darkens it. Haemolytic jaundice raises the unconjugated fraction, which cannot enter urine, and gives dark stools rather than pale ones.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pf-endocrine-renal-disorders-3`,topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In primary hypothyroidism due to Hashimoto thyroiditis, the expected laboratory pattern is`,options:[`T4 low, TSH low`,`T4 low, TSH high`,`T4 high, TSH low`,`T4 normal, TSH low`],answer:1,explanation:`A failing thyroid gland produces less T4, and the loss of negative feedback lets the pituitary raise TSH, so TSH is the most sensitive early marker. Low T4 with low TSH would instead indicate secondary hypothyroidism from pituitary failure.`},{id:`q-pf-endocrine-renal-disorders-4`,topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Hyperpigmentation of skin creases and buccal mucosa in Addison disease is due to`,options:[`Iron deposition in the skin`,`Direct action of aldosterone on melanocytes`,`Excess melatonin from the pineal gland`,`Raised ACTH and MSH derived from pro-opiomelanocortin`],answer:3,explanation:`Low cortisol removes negative feedback, so the pituitary secretes large amounts of POMC-derived ACTH and MSH, which stimulate melanocytes. This is why pigmentation is a feature of primary adrenal failure but is absent in secondary failure, where ACTH is low.`},{id:`q-pf-endocrine-renal-disorders-5`,topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which finding best distinguishes nephritic from nephrotic syndrome?`,options:[`Raised serum creatinine`,`Generalised oedema`,`Red blood cell casts with hypertension`,`Proteinuria of any degree`],answer:2,explanation:`Red cell casts indicate glomerular inflammation and bleeding, and together with hypertension and mild proteinuria define the nephritic picture. Oedema and proteinuria occur in both syndromes, so neither can discriminate on its own.`},{id:`q-pf-endocrine-renal-disorders-6`,topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Helicobacter pylori survives in the acidic stomach chiefly because it produces`,options:[`Urease`,`Coagulase`,`Beta-lactamase`,`Catalase`],answer:0,explanation:`Urease splits urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide, creating an alkaline microenvironment around the organism; this is also the basis of the rapid urease test and the urea breath test. Catalase is produced too but only helps it evade oxidative killing, not acid.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pf-endocrine-renal-disorders-7`,topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient in diabetic ketoacidosis has Na+ 135, Cl- 95 and HCO3- 10 mEq/L. The anion gap is`,options:[`40 mEq/L`,`10 mEq/L`,`20 mEq/L`,`30 mEq/L`],answer:3,explanation:`Anion gap = 135 - (95 + 10) = 30 mEq/L, well above the normal 8 to 12, confirming a high anion gap metabolic acidosis from accumulated ketoacids. Reading 20 usually comes from subtracting only the chloride and forgetting the bicarbonate.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-pf-endocrine-renal-disorders-8`,topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient with oliguria has urine sodium of 12 mEq/L and urine osmolality of 620 mOsm/kg. This pattern indicates`,options:[`Chronic glomerulonephritis`,`Prerenal azotaemia`,`Acute tubular necrosis`,`Postrenal obstruction`],answer:1,explanation:`Intact tubules responding to hypoperfusion conserve sodium avidly and concentrate the urine, giving urine Na+ below 20 mEq/L and osmolality above 500 mOsm/kg. In acute tubular necrosis the damaged tubules cannot do either, so urine sodium exceeds 40 mEq/L and osmolality falls below 350 mOsm/kg.`},{id:`q-pf-neoplasia-infectious-1`,topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The single feature that unequivocally identifies a tumour as malignant is`,options:[`Metastasis`,`Presence of necrosis`,`Rapid growth`,`Large size`],answer:0,explanation:`Metastasis is the one property no benign tumour ever shows, so its presence proves malignancy. Rapid growth, large size and necrosis all raise suspicion but each can occur in benign lesions such as a large uterine leiomyoma with degeneration.`},{id:`q-pf-neoplasia-infectious-2`,topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following tumours is malignant despite the suffix -oma?`,options:[`Lipoma`,`Adenoma`,`Melanoma`,`Fibroma`],answer:2,explanation:`Melanoma, along with lymphoma, myeloma and seminoma, is malignant despite the benign-sounding suffix. Lipoma, adenoma and fibroma follow the usual rule that -oma alone denotes a benign neoplasm.`},{id:`q-pf-neoplasia-infectious-3`,topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The most frequently mutated gene in human cancers, known as the guardian of the genome, is`,options:[`MYC`,`p53`,`BCL-2`,`RAS`],answer:1,explanation:`p53 arrests the cell cycle at G1 for repair and triggers apoptosis if the damage is irreparable, and it is mutated in more than half of all human cancers; germline loss causes Li-Fraumeni syndrome. RAS is the most commonly mutated proto-oncogene, which is a different question from the most commonly mutated gene overall.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-pf-neoplasia-infectious-4`,topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Sarcomas most characteristically metastasise by which route?`,options:[`Transcoelomic`,`Perineural`,`Lymphatic`,`Haematogenous`],answer:3,explanation:`Sarcomas invade thin-walled veins and spread through the bloodstream, typically to lung and liver. Lymphatic spread to regional nodes is the pattern typical of carcinomas, and reversing the two is the commonest error on this question.`},{id:`q-pf-neoplasia-infectious-5`,topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Relapse of malaria months after apparently successful treatment occurs with`,options:[`All four species equally`,`Plasmodium falciparum only`,`Plasmodium vivax and Plasmodium ovale`,`Plasmodium malariae only`],answer:2,explanation:`Only vivax and ovale form dormant hypnozoites in hepatocytes, so radical cure needs primaquine in addition to a blood schizonticide. Falciparum has no liver stage after treatment; its late reappearance is recrudescence from persisting blood forms, not true relapse.`},{id:`q-pf-neoplasia-infectious-6`,topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Intestinal perforation in typhoid fever typically occurs in which week of illness, and at which site?`,options:[`Third week, at the Peyer patches of the terminal ileum`,`Fourth week, in the duodenum`,`First week, in the stomach`,`Second week, in the colon`],answer:0,explanation:`Hyperplastic Peyer patches ulcerate along the long axis of the terminal ileum and perforate or bleed in the third week. Blood culture is the diagnostic test of the first week, while stool culture and the Widal test become useful later, which is a separate timeline worth keeping distinct.`},{id:`q-pf-neoplasia-infectious-7`,topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome produces a fusion gene that acts as`,options:[`A defective DNA repair enzyme`,`An overexpressed anti-apoptotic protein`,`An inactivated tumour suppressor`,`A constitutively active tyrosine kinase`],answer:3,explanation:`BCR-ABL encodes a tyrosine kinase that is permanently switched on, driving unregulated proliferation in chronic myeloid leukaemia, and it is the target of imatinib. Overexpression of an anti-apoptotic protein describes t(14;18) with BCL-2 in follicular lymphoma, a different mechanism entirely.`},{id:`q-pf-neoplasia-infectious-8`,topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Hyponatraemia due to ectopic antidiuretic hormone secretion is most characteristically a paraneoplastic feature of`,options:[`Squamous cell carcinoma of the lung`,`Small cell carcinoma of the lung`,`Renal cell carcinoma`,`Hepatocellular carcinoma`],answer:1,explanation:`Small cell carcinoma is of neuroendocrine origin and secretes ADH, giving SIADH, and also ACTH, giving ectopic Cushing syndrome. Squamous cell carcinoma of the lung is the tempting alternative but secretes PTH-related peptide, producing hypercalcaemia rather than hyponatraemia.`}],n=[{id:`f-pf-cell-injury-inflammation-1`,topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,front:`Five cardinal signs of inflammation?`,back:`Rubor, calor, tumor, dolor, functio laesa - redness, heat, swelling, pain, loss of function.`},{id:`f-pf-cell-injury-inflammation-2`,topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,front:`Which necrosis occurs in the brain, and which in tuberculosis?`,back:`Liquefactive in brain infarct; caseous in tuberculosis.`},{id:`f-pf-cell-injury-inflammation-3`,topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,front:`Three nuclear changes of necrosis?`,back:`Pyknosis (shrinkage), karyorrhexis (fragmentation), karyolysis (dissolution).`},{id:`f-pf-cell-injury-inflammation-4`,topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,front:`Exudate versus transudate on protein content?`,back:`Exudate above 3 g/dL protein, specific gravity above 1.020; transudate below 3 g/dL, below 1.012.`},{id:`f-pf-cell-injury-inflammation-5`,topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,front:`Keloid versus hypertrophic scar?`,back:`Keloid extends beyond the wound margin and recurs; hypertrophic scar stays within it and regresses.`},{id:`f-pf-cell-injury-inflammation-6`,topicId:`pf-cell-injury-inflammation`,front:`Which shock types are distributive with low peripheral resistance?`,back:`Septic, anaphylactic and neurogenic shock.`},{id:`f-pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders-1`,topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,front:`Order of appearance of cardiac markers after MI?`,back:`Myoglobin (1 to 2 h), then CK-MB and troponin (3 to 6 h), then LDH (10 h).`},{id:`f-pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders-2`,topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,front:`Which marker detects re-infarction and why?`,back:`CK-MB, because it normalises in 48 to 72 hours while troponin stays raised for 7 to 10 days.`},{id:`f-pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders-3`,topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,front:`Virchow triad for thrombosis?`,back:`Endothelial injury, abnormal blood flow (stasis or turbulence), hypercoagulability.`},{id:`f-pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders-4`,topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,front:`Blue bloater versus pink puffer?`,back:`Blue bloater is chronic bronchitis with cyanosis and oedema; pink puffer is emphysema with dyspnoea and pursed lip breathing.`},{id:`f-pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders-5`,topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,front:`Four stages of lobar pneumonia?`,back:`Congestion, red hepatisation, grey hepatisation, resolution.`},{id:`f-pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders-6`,topicId:`pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders`,front:`What is the Ghon complex?`,back:`The primary tuberculous subpleural focus plus involved hilar lymph nodes.`},{id:`f-pf-endocrine-renal-disorders-1`,topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,front:`Diagnostic cut-offs for diabetes mellitus?`,back:`Fasting glucose 126 mg/dL or more, 2-hour value 200 mg/dL or more, or HbA1c 6.5 percent or more.`},{id:`f-pf-endocrine-renal-disorders-2`,topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,front:`What does HbA1c reflect?`,back:`Mean plasma glucose over the preceding 8 to 12 weeks, the lifespan of the red cell.`},{id:`f-pf-endocrine-renal-disorders-3`,topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,front:`Which bilirubin fraction appears in urine, and why?`,back:`Only the conjugated fraction, because it is water soluble. Haemolytic jaundice is therefore acholuric.`},{id:`f-pf-endocrine-renal-disorders-4`,topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,front:`Electrolyte pattern of Addison disease?`,back:`Low sodium, high potassium, low glucose, low blood pressure - the mirror image of Cushing syndrome.`},{id:`f-pf-endocrine-renal-disorders-5`,topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,front:`Nephrotic syndrome proteinuria threshold?`,back:`More than 3.5 g per day, with hypoalbuminaemia, oedema and hyperlipidaemia.`},{id:`f-pf-endocrine-renal-disorders-6`,topicId:`pf-endocrine-renal-disorders`,front:`How does H. pylori resist gastric acid?`,back:`Urease splits urea to ammonia, alkalinising its microenvironment - the basis of the urea breath test.`},{id:`f-pf-neoplasia-infectious-1`,topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,front:`Grading versus staging?`,back:`Grade is microscopic differentiation; stage is extent of spread by TNM. Stage predicts survival better.`},{id:`f-pf-neoplasia-infectious-2`,topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,front:`Metastatic route of carcinoma versus sarcoma?`,back:`Carcinoma spreads mainly by lymphatics; sarcoma mainly by blood.`},{id:`f-pf-neoplasia-infectious-3`,topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,front:`Knudson two-hit hypothesis in one line?`,back:`Both alleles of a tumour suppressor must be lost; an inherited first hit makes tumours early and bilateral.`},{id:`f-pf-neoplasia-infectious-4`,topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,front:`Tumour marker for hepatocellular carcinoma and for prostate?`,back:`Alpha-fetoprotein for hepatocellular carcinoma; PSA for prostate.`},{id:`f-pf-neoplasia-infectious-5`,topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,front:`CD4 count that defines AIDS?`,back:`Below 200 cells per microlitre, or the presence of an AIDS-defining illness.`},{id:`f-pf-neoplasia-infectious-6`,topicId:`pf-neoplasia-infectious`,front:`Which hepatitis viruses become chronic, and which is dangerous in pregnancy?`,back:`B, C and D can become chronic; hepatitis E carries high mortality in pregnancy.`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};